  "cli_result_text": "Files submitted. Summary of the traced flow and root cause:\n\n**1. FastAPI: request body \u2192 validated args \u2192 handler**\n- `fastapi/routing.py` (`get_request_handler`) reads the raw JSON body off the `Request` and calls `solve_dependencies`.\n- `fastapi/dependencies/utils.py`:\n  - `solve_dependencies()` passes the parsed body dict to `request_body_to_args()`.\n  - `request_body_to_args()` pulls the value for the body field(s) and calls `field.validate(value, values, loc=loc)` \u2014 this is the FastAPI\u2192Pydantic boundary.\n  - `get_body_field()` / `get_dependant()` / `analyze_param()` establish how the endpoint's Pydantic model becomes a body `ModelField` in the first place.\n- `fastapi/_compat.py`: the Pydantic-v2 `ModelField.validate()` shim wraps the field's annotation in a `pydantic.TypeAdapter` and calls `self._type_adapter.validate_python(value, from_attributes=True)` \u2014 this is where control passes into Pydantic-core's compiled validator for the model. (Errors from here become the 422; if no exception is raised, whatever `validate_python` returns \u2014 including a field silently defaulted to `None` \u2014 is accepted as success.)\n\n**2. Pydantic v2: discriminated union + `model_validator(mode='before')`**\n- `pydantic/_internal/_discriminated_union.py` (`apply_discriminator` / `_ApplyInferredDiscriminator`) converts a `Union[...]` field annotated with a discriminator into a `tagged-union` core schema that dispatches on a literal key read directly out of the incoming dict for *that field*.\n- `pydantic/_internal/_generate_schema.py`:\n  - `_model_schema()` builds the model's core schema (a `model-fields`/`model` schema containing that tagged-union as one field's schema).\n  - It then calls `apply_model_validators(schema, model_validators, 'outer')`.\n  - `apply_model_validators()` (chunk 33-34): for a validator with `info.mode == 'before'`, when `mode == 'outer'` it wraps the *entire* model schema in a `function-before` schema \u2014 meaning the before-validator's return value is what the model-fields schema (and therefore the nested discriminated-union field) actually sees. It runs strictly before any field-level or discriminator logic.\n- `pydantic/functional_validators.py` (`model_validator`, `ModelBeforeValidator*` protocols) is where the decorator is defined and where its mode is recorded via `_decorators.ModelValidatorDecoratorInfo(mode=mode)`.\n- `pydantic/main.py` `model_validate` / `model_validate_json` are the public entry points that invoke the compiled `SchemaValidator` (built from the schema graph above) against the raw dict/JSON.\n\n**3. Why the field silently becomes `None` with no 422**\n\nA `model_validator(mode='before')` on the request-body model is compiled as the outermost `function-before` wrapper around the whole model schema (`_generate_schema.apply_model_validators`, `'outer'` + `mode='before'`). Its output dict is what the model-fields schema \u2014 and the nested `tagged-union` schema for the discriminated field \u2014 actually validates. If that before-hook reshapes/filters the incoming payload (e.g., a v1-style root-validator ported over that rebuilds the dict from `cls.model_fields`/known keys, or renames/strips keys) and in doing so drops the discriminator key (or its alias) from the sub-object, two things follow:\n\n- `_ApplyInferredDiscriminator`/the compiled tagged-union schema never gets to run its dispatch logic at all for that field, because the field's key is now absent from the dict entirely.\n- Since the union field is optional (has a default, typically `None`), an absent key is legitimate \"field not provided\" \u2014 it's filled from `FieldInfo`'s default rather than raising `union_tag_not_found`/`missing`.\n\nSo the failure isn't a broken discriminator matcher \u2014 it's that `apply_model_validators`'s `mode='before'` wrapping sits *upstream* of the discriminator dispatch in the schema tree, so a before-hook that strips the discriminator key makes the field look \"not sent\" rather than \"sent with a bad/unmatched discriminator.\" That's why FastAPI's `ModelField.validate()` \u2192 `TypeAdapter.validate_python()` returns cleanly (200 OK) with the field defaulted to `None` instead of raising a `ValidationError` that FastAPI would otherwise turn into a 422.",
